    "https://careers.umich.edu/career-development": {
      "title": "Career Development",
      "description": "Career Development | U-M Careers Skip to main content twitter rss addthis Utility Login Help and FAQ Career Development Main navigation Home Search Jobs Benefits Choose Michigan Temporary Employment Student Employment Career Development Help and FAQ Building your career at Michigan begins with knowing the right resources — here are three, to help you get started. The first one helps you through the steps you need to facilitate your professional growth. The second, the Career Path Navigator, illustrates real career paths that people have taken at the University of Michigan. The third is designed to assist the partners of U‑M faculty or staff members with a variety of job search resources. Additional Resources links to a compilation of valuable Web sites. Resources for Career Development University Human Resources provides resources for Career Development including career self-assessment, university resources, job search tools and career planning information. Career Path Navigator Designed as a general guide to assist in your personal career planning, the Career Path Navigator shows how positions at U‑M relate to other careers in the university. It helps to show the potential career path for a specific job. Additionally, you can see actual career transitions (promotions as well as transitioning careers) made by university staff members. Resources for Partners Whether you are considering a move to the University of Michigan or you are already in the Ann Arbor area, University Human Resources provides job search information and resources for partners of U‑M faculty or staff members . The Office of the Provost also provides Dual Career Services to assist partners of tenure-track and tenured faculty. External Candidate Resources Michigan Commission for the Blind (MCB) Central Office email 201 N. Washington, 2nd Floor P.O. Box 30652 Lansing, MI 48909 email (517) 373-2062 email (800) 292-4200 email (517) 335-5140 Detroit Regional Office email Cadillac Place 3038 W. Grand Blvd. Suite 4-450 Detroit, MI 48202-6038 email (313) 456-1646 email (313) 456-1645 email http://www.michigan.gov/mcb The MCB provides services to help visually impaired individuals gain employment. Michigan Rehabilitation Services (MRS) - Washtenaw County Main Office email 3810 Packard Rd. Suite 170 Ann Arbor, MI 48108 email (734) 677-1125 email (877) 901-9195 email [email protected] email https://www.michigan.gov/leo/bureaus-agencies/mrs/individuals/individualized-services MRS provides services to assist individuals with disabilities succeed in the workplace. Michigan Works! email 304 Harriet St. Ypsilanti, MI 48197 email (734) 714-9814 email https://www.mwse.org/jobseekers/ Michigan Works offers services to individuals who are currently underemployed, unemployed or looking for a career change. The Women's Center of Southeastern Michigan email 1100 Victors Way #10 Ann Arbor, MI 48108-5220 email (734) 973-6779 email [email protected] email https://womenscentersemi.org/services/career-coaching/ The Women's Center provides low-cost group and individual Job Coaching services for women. Ypsilanti District Library (YDL) email 5577 Whittaker Road Ypsilanti, MI 48197 email 229 W Michigan Avenue Ypsilanti, MI 48197 email 1900 Harris Road Ypsilanti, MI 48198 email (734) 482-4110 email https://www.ypsilibrary.org/services/in-our-libraries/computer-classes/ email https://www.ypsilibrary.org/interests/job-seekers-skill-builders/ The Ypsilanti District Library offers free group, 1:1, and self-guided online computer courses. The library also has events for job seekers and skill building. Adult Education Programs email [email protected] email http://www.michigan.gov/adulteducation Connects adults interested in gaining education levels equal to high school graduates with area resources. The Ann Arbor Center for Independent Living (AACIL) email 3941 Research Drive Ann Arbor, MI 48108 email (734) 971-0277 email (734) 971-0826 email http://www.aacil.org/ The AACIL offers a variety of employment services for individuals with disabilities. Ann Arbor District Library (AADL) email 343 S. Fifth Ave. (Downtown Library) email 2359 Oak Valley Dr. (Pittsfield Branch) email 2713 Plymouth Rd. (Northeast Branch) email 3000 Huron Pkwy. (Traverwood Branch) email 3090 E. Eisenhower Pkwy. (Malletts Creek Branch) email 2503 Jackson Ave. (West Branch) email (734) 327-4200 email http://www.aadl.org/ Microsoft Office, Mac, Job Search and Internet classes are available at the AADL. A library card/membership is not required. Registration is not required. And the classes are available at no charge. Go to aadl.org/classes for the schedule. Center for the Education of Women (CEW)-University of Michigan email 330 E. Liberty St. Ann Arbor, MI 48104 email (734) 764-6360 email [email protected] email https://cew.umich.edu/ The CEW offers individual counseling and presents a broad array of career development workshops. Topics include the art of networking; interviewing tips and business etiquette; resume and cover preparation, career decision-making and the art of negotiation. The Information Center, Inc. (a.k.a. The Family Resource Place) email (734) 282-7171 email [email protected] email http://www.theinfocenter.info/ Certified Work Force Development Professionals connects Michigan job seekers with an assortment of no-cost community employment resources. Jewish Family Services of Washtenaw County (JFS) email 2245 S. State St. Ann Arbor, MI 48104 email (734) 769-0209 email (734) 769-0224 email [email protected] email http://www.jfsannarbor.org/ The JFS Career Services center offers resume writing and job placement assistance, vocational skill assessments, job counseling and occupational skills training for job seekers. Contact Footer Non-discrimination Statement Accessibility Security and Safety Annual Report Labor Law Notices Department of Labor Notices of Filing © 2026 The Regents of the University of Michigan"
    },
